providerResponses.text:
{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You finish one integrated circuit-method task: for each of a handful of unfamiliar circuits you name the applicable law and why, solve it correctly, and check the answers, so the stopping point is a clean method-selection result on changed work rather than more practice."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "EXAM",
          "outcome": "I can explain voltage and current but choose the wrong law on unfamiliar circuits. Focus on selecting and applying the method.",
          "explanation": "The accepted weakness is method selection, not conceptual understanding of voltage and current. The learner already explains those concepts, so no separate relearning phase is justified; the useful work is one integrated task that forces a method choice, a valid selection reason, and a correct solution on changed circuits. Readiness evidence is empty, so the task is COMMITTED but small, and further remediation stays CONTINGENT on an observed failure rather than being booked now.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 45,
            "likelyMinutes": 60,
            "highMinutes": 90
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "method_selection",
              "title": "Method selection on unfamiliar circuits",
              "outcome": "Correctly choose an applicable law, justify the choice, and solve changed circuits without assistance.",
              "readinessCriterion": "The learner names the law, states why its assumptions/topology apply, and gets the correct solution on the changed circuits."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The single integrated task addresses method selection directly by requiring a named law, a valid selection reason, and a correct solution on changed circuits."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "method_task",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "method_selection"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Select and apply the right circuit law on unfamiliar circuits",
            "activityType": "integrated method-selection and problem-solving task",
            "purpose": "Force an explicit method choice with a valid reason and a correct solution on changed circuits, which is the accepted weakness.",
            "instructions": "Use only the circuit laws and methods you have actually been taught. For each of these unfamiliar circuits, first write which law or method you will use and why its assumptions and topology apply, then solve it and check your answer. Do not use advanced methods you have not been taught; if a circuit seems to need one, say so instead of guessing.\n\nCircuit A: a series circuit with a 12 V source and resistors 4 ohm, 6 ohm, and 2 ohm. Find the current and the voltage across the 6 ohm resistor.\n\nCircuit B: a parallel circuit with a 9 V source and two resistors, 3 ohm and 6 ohm, in parallel. Find the total current and the current through the 6 ohm resistor.\n\nCircuit C: a circuit with a 10 V source, a 5 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair of 10 ohm and 10 ohm. Find the total current and the voltage across the parallel pair.\n\nCircuit D: a circuit with a 6 V source and resistors 2 ohm and 4 ohm in series, but the 4 ohm resistor is replaced by an unknown R. If the current is 0.5 A, find R.\n\nAfter solving, check each answer by substituting back into the law you chose. If any answer is wrong, correct it and explain the actual error in your method choice or application.",
            "completionCriterion": "For each circuit, the learner names an applicable law, gives a valid reason its assumptions/topology apply, and produces the correct solution; any wrong answers are corrected with the actual error explained.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 45,
              "likelyMinutes": 60,
              "highMinutes": 90
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 60,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "No separate conceptual relearning of voltage and current is booked because the learner already explains them; that time is left unbooked rather than filled with redundant work."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Readiness evidence is empty, so the task is sized as a small integrated check; if it fails, a contingent bounded remediation can be added later."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop after the integrated task if the learner correctly selects and applies methods on the changed circuits; do not add a second check or a repeated set without a concrete failure."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The accepted outcome is method selection, not concept learning, so the plan trades a broader set of practice problems for one integrated task that directly tests choosing a law, justifying it, and solving changed circuits. Finishing that task correctly is the bounded stopping point; extra practice or a second check is not booked because it would not add distinct evidence unless the first task shows a concrete failure."
}

