            "localId": "learn_bayes",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_learn"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2"
            ],
            "title": "Learn base rate vs sensitivity and derive Bayes' rule",
            "activityType": "Guided explanation and derivation",
            "purpose": "Directly resolve the base-rate/sensitivity confusion while constructing the formula that the changed example will use.",
            "instructions": "Work through a short explanation of a medical test. Define four quantities explicitly: prevalence P(D) (the base rate), sensitivity P(+|D), specificity P(-|no D), and the false positive rate. Write each in words and symbols, then build a 2x2 table for a concrete example with prevalence 1% and sensitivity 90% to see how a positive result can still be uncommon. Derive Bayes' rule P(D|+) = P(+|D)P(D) / [P(+|D)P(D) + P(+|no D)P(no D)] by substituting the law of total probability, and say in one sentence why the base rate appears in the numerator. Then write a one-paragraph explanation in your own words of why the base rate and sensitivity are different quantities and how confusing them changes the answer. Use only the probability tools you have been taught; do not assume advanced methods. Finish by writing the formula from memory and checking it against the derivation.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written the four quantities with correct labels, derived Bayes' rule, and written a paragraph that correctly distinguishes base rate from sensitivity without swapping them.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 25,
              "likelyMinutes": 35,
              "highMinutes": 50
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 40,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          },
          {
            "localId": "changed_example",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_apply"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Solve and explain a changed medical-test example independently",
            "activityType": "Independent application and explanation",
            "purpose": "Demonstrates transfer to an unfamiliar example and checks that the base rate is no longer confused with sensitivity.",
            "instructions": "Without re-reading the derivation, solve this changed example: a disease has prevalence 2%. A test has sensitivity 95% and specificity 90%. A patient tests positive. Compute the posterior probability that the patient has the disease using Bayes' rule, showing the numerator and denominator separately. Then write two or three sentences explaining what the result says and why a positive test does not imply a 95% chance of disease. Finally, identify which number in the problem is the base rate and which is the sensitivity, and state what happens to the posterior if the base rate were much higher, keeping the test characteristics fixed. Use only methods you have been taught; if you find yourself wanting an advanced technique you have not learned, note it and continue with the taught method.",
            "completionCriterion": "The posterior is computed correctly, the explanation correctly attributes the result to the low base rate, and the base rate and sensitivity are identified without being swapped.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 20,
              "likelyMinutes": 30,
              "highMinutes": 40
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 35,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "learn_bayes"
            ]
          },
          {
            "localId": "remediation",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_apply"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2"
            ],
            "title": "Targeted remediation if the changed example reveals confusion",
            "activityType": "Failure-triggered correction",
            "purpose": "Repairs the specific misunderstanding only if the changed example shows the base rate and sensitivity are still conflated.",
            "instructions": "Re-read your changed-example solution. Identify the exact step where the base rate and sensitivity were swapped or where the denominator omitted a term. Rewrite that step using the 2x2 table method from the lesson, then recompute the posterior. State in one sentence what the base rate contributes to the denominator.",
            "completionCriterion": "The corrected step uses the base rate and sensitivity in their proper roles and the recomputed posterior is correct.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 10,
              "likelyMinutes": 15,
              "highMinutes": 20
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 20,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "CONTINGENT",
              "trigger": {
                "checkpointActivityId": "changed_example",
                "condition": "The posterior is incorrect or the explanation swaps the base rate and sensitivity."
              }
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "changed_example"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "The changed example uses a different prevalence and test characteristics than the lesson so it tests transfer rather than recall; this is a deliberate educational choice, not extra practice for its own sake.",
            "Remediation is contingent on observed failure in the changed example to avoid unnecessary repetition for a learner who already demonstrates correct application."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "No prior readiness evidence exists, so the lesson includes a brief diagnostic element rather than assuming prior mastery."
