{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You can explain Bayes' rule in your own words, correctly compute the posterior for a changed medical-test example while explicitly distinguishing the base rate (prevalence) from sensitivity, and state why the two are different quantities."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "MASTERY",
          "outcome": "Develop the ability to explain Bayes' rule and apply it to a changed medical-test probability example, with the base rate no longer confused with sensitivity.",
          "explanation": "The reported weakness is a specific conceptual conflation: treating prevalence (base rate) as if it were sensitivity. Since there is no demonstrated readiness yet, the smallest useful strategy is one integrated work block that (a) explains the distinction between prior/base rate, sensitivity and false-positive rate, (b) works a concrete medical-test example with the actual numbers, and (c) transfers the same reasoning to a changed example. Explanation, supported application and an independent changed example are integrated rather than split into ceremonial stages. Further practice or a second check is left contingent on a concrete observed error in the changed example, so no quota of drills is imposed.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 40,
            "likelyMinutes": 60,
            "highMinutes": 90
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_understand",
              "title": "Understand and apply Bayes' rule to a changed example",
              "outcome": "Explain Bayes' rule, distinguish base rate from sensitivity, and correctly solve a changed medical-test posterior.",
              "readinessCriterion": "Explains the rule in own words and computes the posterior for a changed example with the base rate and sensitivity correctly identified and used."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Explains base rates and conditional probability as the foundation for distinguishing prevalence from test accuracy."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Provides the Bayes' rule statement and the computation used on the worked and changed examples."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Requires applying the rule to an unfamiliar medical-test example, evidencing transfer rather than recall."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "bayes_integrated",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_understand"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Explain Bayes' rule and solve a changed medical-test example",
            "activityType": "Integrated explanation and applied problem solving",
            "purpose": "Resolve the base-rate/sensitivity confusion by explaining the distinction and applying Bayes' rule to a changed example.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in one sitting. (1) Write the definition of each quantity in a medical test: prevalence (base rate) = P(D), sensitivity = P(+|D), false-positive rate = P(+|not D). In your own words, state why the base rate is not the same as sensitivity. (2) State Bayes' rule for P(D|+) in terms of these quantities. (3) Worked example: prevalence 1%, sensitivity 90%, false-positive rate 5%. Compute P(D|+) by hand, showing the numerator P(+|D)P(D) and denominator P(+|D)P(D) + P(+|not D)P(not D). Check your arithmetic. (4) Changed example: prevalence 10%, sensitivity 80%, false-positive rate 10%. Before computing, write which number is the base rate and which is the sensitivity, then compute P(D|+). (5) Compare the two results and explain, in terms of the base rate, why the posterior differs even though sensitivity is similar. Use only the taught approach; if you are unsure of any step, say so explicitly rather than guessing. If your changed-example computation is wrong, identify the specific step where the error occurred (for example, using sensitivity where the base rate belongs) and redo that step.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written the correct posterior for both examples with the base rate and sensitivity correctly identified, and you can explain in your own words why the base rate is not the sensitivity.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 40,
              "likelyMinutes": 60,
              "highMinutes": 90
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 60,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "One integrated activity is used instead of separate explain/practice/check stages, because the weakness is a specific conceptual confusion that explanation plus a changed example can address directly; this avoids unnecessary repetition."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Whether the learner can complete the changed example without assistance is not yet known; the contingent activity is available if the changed example reveals a concrete error."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when the changed example is solved correctly with base rate and sensitivity correctly distinguished and the explanation is given; do not add further practice unless a concrete error is observed."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The plan commits one focused integrated activity rather than a multi-stage sequence or a large practice set. This honors the mastery policy by requiring explanation and transfer on a changed example, while avoiding ritual repetition. A contingent activity is included only as a bounded alternative if the committed changed example reveals a concrete error, so no extra work is imposed without observed failure."
}

